File handling
-we need to handle the file to overcome from data loss file can be handle in two ways:
1.Text file
-store data in form of characters
2.Binary file
-store data in byte(group of 8-bits)
-eg:image,audio,video

#TEXT File
1.Opening file in python
syntax:
open(file_name,mode to open)

Modes:r(read),x(create),w(write),a(append)
-to work with text file mode:t(text)
-to work with binary file mode:b(binary)
-'rt':this two modes are default

*when we open file it give reference or pointer to beginning of that file assigning on a variable we can edit the file
*every time when we open file we should close the file if not it may lead to file corrupted

#Closing a file
-close()
-variable name.close()

*after closing a file we cannot perform any operation

2.Creating file
syntax:
open("File name","tx")
mode=x,for txt "xt",for binary "bx"

#writing into file
-write(content)

3.The W mode
syntax:
open("File name","wt")
-open the file for writing /overwrites the file
-it deletes the content of the existing file
-but if file does not exist create new file/using x mode is safe for creating new file
-uses:write(content)-to write into file

4.Reading files
-open("File name","rt")
-read()=reads content of the file, if files open in text(t) mode it reads context as string
-read(10)=it will only read first 10 character of content
-readline()=it will read first line for another line we have to use readline() next to it
-readline()=it will now read second line but i give index number it will read char

#readlines()=it read all the line at once and give in list

5.Appending into a file-mode:a
-to add content at last of existing file
-if file does not exist it create ne file

#c,w,a
uses:write()
#r
uses:1.read(n)
     2.readline()
     3.readlines()

6.With statement
-The with statement is used in file handling mainly because it automatically closes the file after you're finished with it.
-if file open in read mode and write function is use it close the file automatically likewise
syntax:
with open("file name","tr/mode") as fh: